from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path

g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']

C('github','GitHub hosts Git projects and tools for working together. Learn repository pages, issues, pull requests and checks through written examples that need no account.','These are worked plans, not commands that change a hosted project. You can read many public GitHub pages without an account. Publishing or changing a real project is a separate action on that service.','https://docs.github.com/en/get-started',[
L('Git and GitHub have different jobs','Git records file history on a computer. GitHub hosts Git repositories and adds pages for collaboration. You can learn local Git without a GitHub account.','Local computer: edit files and record Git commits\nGitHub repository: share selected commits and review proposed changes','The local and hosted copies are separate places. A local commit does not upload itself to GitHub.','Explain where a commit made without internet is stored.','Do not confuse an account on a hosting service with local version history.','Does a local commit automatically upload to GitHub?','No','Yes'),
L('Find your way around a repository','A GitHub repository page has areas for code and collaboration. Start with its README, then inspect the files and recent changes.','Code: files and README\nIssues: problems and ideas\nPull requests: proposed code changes\nActions: configured automated runs','Each area answers a different question. To understand what a project does, the README is usually the best starting point.','Choose where you would look for a proposed spelling fix.','A public repository can still contain code that is unsafe to run; read setup steps before using them.','Where do proposed code changes appear?','Pull requests','Only the README'),
L('Follow the GitHub Flow','GitHub Flow is a lightweight way to work on changes. A focused branch and pull request let people inspect an idea before it joins the main branch.','Create a branch -> make focused commits -> open a pull request -> review and check -> merge when ready','The change has a review stage between writing code and combining it with main. Exact project rules decide who may merge.','Write a branch name for correcting a search label.','Do not combine unrelated unfinished features into one small review.','What happens before merging in this flow?','Review and checks','History is erased'),
L('Write an issue someone can reproduce','A GitHub issue can record a bug or proposed task. A useful bug report gives steps, expected behaviour and actual behaviour.','Title: Search misses lowercase moon\nSteps: type moon in the book search\nExpected: Moon appears\nActual: no matching book appears','A maintainer can repeat the exact steps. The issue describes a problem; it does not itself change the search code.','Add a browser detail if it affects the bug.','Never put passwords or private learner records into a public issue.','Which detail helps reproduce the bug?','The exact search text','Only a request to fix everything'),
L('Explain a pull request','A pull request, often called a PR, proposes combining changes from one branch into another. Its description should explain the result for a reviewer.','Base branch: main\nHead branch: fix-search-case\nProblem: lowercase queries missed titles\nChange: compare title and query in lowercase\nCheck: Moon and moon both match','The base receives the proposed changes. The head holds the change being reviewed. The explanation links the edit to a visible behaviour.','Write a one-sentence PR description for a spelling correction.','Opening a PR does not mean it has been approved or merged.','Which branch receives the proposed change?','The base branch','The head branch automatically'),
L('Use the Files changed view','The Files changed view shows the difference a PR proposes. Review the actual lines and ask focused questions about behaviour and edge cases.','Old: title === query\nNew: title.toLowerCase() === query.toLowerCase()\nReview question: should outside spaces also be ignored?','The edit ignores letter case, but it does not trim spaces. The review question identifies another case that needs a decision.','Suggest a check using a query with a trailing space.','A small diff can still change important behaviour; read it instead of judging only its size.','What does this change handle?','Letter case','Leading and trailing spaces automatically'),
L('Understand review outcomes','A GitHub review may comment, approve or request changes. Project branch rules determine which reviews are required before merging.','Comment: asks a question or gives feedback\nApprove: reviewer considers the change ready\nRequest changes: reviewer identifies work needed','The outcomes communicate different levels of readiness. A review approval is evidence from a person, not a guarantee that every bug is absent.','Write a clear change request for a missing empty-query test.','Resolve the reason for a review request; do not just hide its comment.','Which outcome says work is needed before approval?','Request changes','Approve'),
L('Read an Actions check','GitHub Actions can run a workflow when code changes. A workflow is a configured set of jobs and steps. Its check reports whether those steps succeeded.','Workflow file: .github/workflows/check.yml\nJob: test\nStep: run the project test command\nResult: passed','The green result says the configured test command passed in that run. It does not prove the workflow checked every browser or every page.','Name one visual check that unit tests may not cover.','Read logs and configuration before relying on a badge whose checks you do not know.','What does a passing check prove?','Its configured checks passed in that run','Every possible feature is correct'),
L('Protect the main branch with rules','Repository rules can require reviews or status checks before a branch accepts changes. This helps a team apply the same process consistently.','Worked rule plan for main:\nRequire a pull request\nRequire the test status check\nRequire a suitable review\nAllow only intended people to bypass rules','The plan records a review process. Available settings depend on the repository and GitHub plan; no rule is changed by reading this lesson.','Explain why a broad bypass permission can weaken the plan.','A rule only helps when it is configured for the intended branch and used consistently.','Why require a status check before merging?','To enforce an agreed automated check','To make review unnecessary'),
L('Fork a project or clone it','A fork is a hosted repository linked to an upstream project. A clone is a local repository copy. A fork can hold proposed contributions without granting write access to upstream.','Upstream project -> your hosted fork\nHosted fork -> local clone\nLocal commits -> proposed change from the fork','The fork lives on GitHub and the clone lives on a computer. Moving commits between them requires separate Git operations.','Draw where files are edited in this flow.','Forking does not remove the original licence or give permission for every use.','Which copy is on the local computer?','The clone','The hosted fork'),
L('Explain a version with a release','A Git tag names a specific point in history. A GitHub release can add notes and downloadable files around a tag.','Tag: v1.1.0\nRelease title: Search improvement\nNotes: search now ignores letter case\nKnown limit: outside spaces are still significant','The notes tell users what changed and what still matters. Later commits are not automatically part of the tagged version.','Write a release note for handling outside spaces in search.','Do not claim a release fixes all bugs when only one behaviour changed.','What does the release note explain?','The version changes and limits','Every future commit'),
L('Read the community files','Community files help people use and contribute to a repository. A licence explains reuse conditions; other files describe participation and reporting.','README.md: purpose and setup\nLICENSE: reuse conditions\nCONTRIBUTING.md: how to propose a change\nCODE_OF_CONDUCT.md: community behaviour\nSECURITY.md: how to report a security issue if provided','These files answer different questions. Read the security reporting instructions before posting sensitive vulnerability details publicly.','Choose which file answers whether you may reuse project code.','Public visibility alone does not mean code has no reuse conditions.','Where do you first check reuse conditions?','LICENSE','Only the repository star count'),
])

C('gitlab','GitLab brings project code, issues, reviews and pipelines together. Learn groups, merge requests, issue boards and checks through small worked plans with clear limits.','These lessons use fictional examples and do not create or change any GitLab resources. Many public pages can be read without signing in. Writing to a real hosted project needs that service and suitable permission.','https://docs.gitlab.com/tutorials/',[
L('Understand a GitLab project','A GitLab project contains a Git repository and related work tools. A group can organise several projects and their access in one place.','Group: school-club\nProject: reading-list\nRepository: program files and Git history\nRelated tools: issues, merge requests and pipelines','The project is more than its files. Its related tools help track work and review changes around the repository.','Explain which part stores the code history.','GitLab can be hosted in different ways, so a project is not always on gitlab.com.','What stores the project code history?','The Git repository','Only the issue board'),
L('Read groups and namespaces','A namespace is a path that helps locate a GitLab project. Groups and subgroups can add levels to that path.','Example path: school-club/web/reading-list\nTop group: school-club\nSubgroup: web\nProject: reading-list','The path shows where the project is organised. Two projects in different namespaces can have the same final project name.','Split the fictional path club/mobile/book-checker into its parts.','A path is not proof that a project is official or trustworthy; inspect its owner and content.','Which part is the project name here?','reading-list','school-club'),
L('Use labels to organise issues','Labels mark kinds of work so issues can be filtered or grouped. A label should have a meaning the team understands.','Issue: Search misses lowercase titles\nLabels: bug, search\nMeaning of bug: current behaviour differs from the intended behaviour','The labels help find related issues. They describe the work; they do not fix the problem or decide its priority by themselves.','Choose a label for adding beginner help text.','Too many overlapping labels can make a board harder to read.','What do labels help with?','Grouping and finding work','Automatically fixing code'),
L('Plan related work with milestones','A milestone groups issues and merge requests around a goal or period. It helps show what work remains for that goal.','Milestone: Reading-list first demo\nIssue 1: show sample books\nIssue 2: add a search box\nIssue 3: check keyboard use','The three tasks support one demo goal. Completing a milestone requires checking the intended outcomes, not only changing task labels.','Add a task that checks an empty search result.','A milestone due date is a plan, not evidence that unfinished work is complete.','What ties these issues together?','The first-demo goal','They all need the same file name'),
L('Make an issue board useful','An issue board shows work in columns based on chosen project rules, often using labels. It helps a team see items waiting or in progress.','Board labels: ready, doing, review\nCard: Add empty-state text\nCurrent label: doing\nNext condition: move to review when the change and checks are ready','The card position communicates a work state. The team needs a clear meaning for each column so movement is useful.','Write a rule for when a card may enter ready.','Moving a card does not change the code or prove its checks passed.','What does moving a card mainly communicate?','A work state','An automatic code deployment'),
L('Prepare a merge request','A merge request, or MR, proposes combining a source branch into a target branch. It connects code changes with review and pipeline results.','Source branch: improve-empty-state\nTarget branch: main\nChange: show No books found when search has no matches\nCheck: search for a title that is absent','The source contains the proposed edit. The target receives it after the project\'s review and merge rules are satisfied.','Write the visible before-and-after behaviour for this change.','A merge request can be a draft while work is unfinished; opening it does not merge it.','Which branch receives the proposed edits?','The target branch','Every branch at once'),
L('Resolve review discussions','Review discussions can point to a specific line or behaviour. Resolving one should mean the concern has been addressed or an agreed decision has been recorded.','Reviewer: what should appear for a query containing only spaces?\nAuthor: treat it as an empty query\nChange: trim the query before filtering\nCheck: add the spaces-only case','The discussion leads to a clear behaviour and a check. The review history explains why trimming was added.','Write a reply when you need more detail to understand a review concern.','Do not mark a discussion resolved only to make an unresolved question disappear.','What makes this discussion ready to resolve?','A clear decision, change and check','The comment has been read once'),
L('Read a CI pipeline file','GitLab CI/CD reads jobs from its pipeline configuration. A job names commands for a runner, which is the worker that executes them.','File: .gitlab-ci.yml\nJob name: test\nStage: test\nScript: npm test','This is a worked description of a job, not executable YAML. The runner needs a suitable environment with the project dependencies installed.','Name one thing a runner needs before it can run npm test.','A job name such as test does not prove meaningful tests were configured.','What executes the job commands?','A runner','The issue label'),
L('Understand stages and jobs','Stages can group pipeline jobs in an order such as build then test. Jobs in one stage may run in parallel when runners are available.','Stage 1: build\n  Job: create app files\nStage 2: test\n  Job: unit checks\n  Job: accessibility checks','The plan places build before the test stage. The two test jobs may run at the same time. Features such as needs can change dependencies in real pipelines.','Explain why a test needing build output must wait for it.','Do not assume listing jobs in a file always makes them run one after another.','Which two jobs could run in parallel here?','Unit and accessibility checks','Build and its dependent checks before build finishes'),
L('Keep useful job artifacts','An artifact is a saved file from a job, such as a test report or built package. It lets later steps or reviewers inspect useful results.','Job: accessibility checks\nArtifact: accessibility-report.json\nPurpose: inspect which pages passed or failed\nRetention: keep only as long as the project needs','The report adds evidence beyond a green or red status. An artifact may contain sensitive data, so choose its contents and access thoughtfully.','Name one useful artifact from a screenshot comparison job.','Do not save secrets or private source data into broadly accessible artifacts.','What is the artifact in this plan?','The saved check report','The name of the project group'),
L('Use protected branches and merge checks','A protected branch restricts who can push or merge. Merge checks can require useful conditions, such as a successful pipeline, before combining code.','Plan for main:\nRestrict direct pushes\nUse merge requests\nRequire the agreed pipeline result\nReview open discussions and required approvals','The plan combines access rules and evidence checks. Available settings vary by GitLab version, plan and project configuration.','Explain why a successful pipeline does not replace reading the diff.','Access permission and review readiness are different questions; both matter.','Why restrict direct pushes to main?','To support the agreed review process','To remove the need for tests'),
L('Read an environment status honestly','An environment represents a place where an application runs, such as a preview or production site. A deployment record links a version to that place.','Environment: preview\nDeployment: commit abc123 from the example project\nChecks: page loads and search works\nLimit: this status does not describe production','The record identifies which version was put in which place. Our example is a written plan and does not deploy anything.','List one check before calling a preview ready for review.','A green deployment job does not prove the running page behaves correctly; inspect it.','What does an environment record help identify?','Where a version is running','Whether all future versions are correct'),
])
